// Boiler, firebox and tender. Source: systems spec B.6 (and B.5 rebuild times at a standstill).
// The model: coal in the firebox burns at (coal / fireTimeConstant) x draft, each unit burned
// raises pressure by steamPerUnit, and the train draws steam for the cylinders (throttle), for
// auxiliaries, and for heating every car. Draft comes from the exhaust, so a standing engine's
// fire burns slower: that is why rebuilding pressure at a stop is slow, and slower for long trains.
// These constants are fitted so the spec's own tables fall out; `dt boiler table` shows the result.
{
  "pressureMax": 100,
  "workingBandMin": 60,
  "workingBandMax": 90,
  "redline": 95,
  // Spec B.6: rupture at 100 held for 20 s.
  "ruptureHoldSeconds": 20,
  // Not in the spec: a safety valve lifts at the redline and sheds up to this much pressure per second, and the lifting
  // valve is itself a loud tell. T109 playtest ("if I red line for too long the boiler should rupture"): at 2.5 it shed
  // whatever any fire could make, and nothing but the Stoker ever reached 100. At 0.6 a hot fire beats it: in the red,
  // vent (or let the fire burn down), or the gauge climbs to 100 and the spec's 20 s there ruptures it.
  "safetyValveLift": 95,
  "safetyValveCapacity": 0.6,
  // Tractive effort fades out below workingBandMin and is gone at this pressure.
  "powerFloor": 20,
  "tenderCapacity": 400,
  "fireboxCapacity": 6,
  "shovelSeconds": 1.2,
  // Note 717 (horseplay; the director, 14 Oct 2026: the lump of coal "is going to need to be able to work with the current
  // coal shoveling system"): this far into a stroke at the firebox (Use held) the coal's on the shovel, and stays on it while
  // Use is held in the cab, turned from the fire; Throw throws it (player.json horseplay.coal) instead of it going in at
  // shovelSeconds. Let go of Use and it's back in the tender, as ever. A hand at the coal face (T29) fills the shovel itself.
  "scoopSeconds": 0.6,
  // App. C.2 "the boiler player's shovel doubling as the crew's best club is intended tension", GDD §12 (note 275): coal
  // goes on with the shovel in hand. There's the one, on the cab's tool rack beside the wrench; Use at the firebox takes it
  // into hand (from your kit, or off the rack if it's there), and Use at the rack with it in hand hangs it back. Taken out
  // to fight with, the fire waits. False: coal goes on by hand, as before. False since note 664 (the director, 13 Oct:
  // "Every crew member should be able to shovel coal regardless of having the shovel in their hand. We shouldn't need a
  // tool to do that"): the shovel stays on its rack as a club.
  "shovelInHand": false,
  // The director's playtest of 13 Oct evening (line 19, note 903): "if I add any coal with any pressure, we should get some
  // level of speed build up ... a shovel and one to three seconds later ... some sort of reaction for the locomotive ... we're
  // going for fun". Main's shovelful just joined the bed and burned over half a minute: nothing to feel. Now this share of it
  // catches (catchSeconds) and flares (flareSeconds), its steam landing as a kick on the gauge that peaks a second after the
  // stroke and is mostly in by three: about +6 on the gauge a shovelful (0.35 x steamPerUnit), the stack and the chuff
  // answering. The same coal makes the same steam, only sooner, so B.6's burn table holds. While it flares the engine
  // surges: surgePerPressure m/s² per unit of pressure a second the flare makes (about 0.3 at a shovelful's peak), at most
  // surgeMax, and only running, from surgeFrom m/s and off the brake: the yard's shunting at walking pace isn't nudged off its
  // marks (StopCrewTests' jackpot carry went astray when it was) and a driver braking in to a stop isn't pushed past it.
  // Not in the spec's tables (systems spec B.6 "A shovelful answers"); fun over realism.
  "catchShare": 0.35,
  "catchSeconds": 0.9,
  "flareSeconds": 1.2,
  "surgePerPressure": 0.15,
  "surgeMax": 0.5,
  "surgeFrom": 4,

  "fireTimeConstant": 30,
  // Draft with the regulator shut (blower only), as a fraction of working draft.
  "idleDraft": 0.6,
  // The blower at a stand (the director, 21 Oct, `nathaniel1-tbm-boiler-pressure-17`, note 969: "for 3 cars the rebuild for
  // any speed from 0 is about 8s, at a full 20 cars the rebuild should be no longer than 30s"): the fire's draft while the
  // engine stands below the working band with the vent shut, raising steam, as a fraction of working draft. Only then:
  // under way (B.6's burn and endurance table), at a stand with steam up (a Stoker's feeding, the hot tub, a stop's winch)
  // and venting (a steam lift) it's idleDraft as ever. A rebuild from nothing to the working band (spec C.2) is 8 s at three cars, 9 at ten, 10 at twenty (were 37, 56
  // and 215 at idleDraft's 0.6).
  "standingDraft": 2.4,
  // The director's playtest of 16 Oct (line 18, note 843): "build pressure a bit faster ... maybe like 15-20% faster". The
  // steam a unit makes and the steam drawn (auxiliaries, heating, the cylinders) all went up 17% together (were 15, 0.05,
  // 0.0675 and 0.5): the gauge moves 17% faster either way, and B.6's burn and endurance table stays as it was, since what
  // burns to hold pressure is draw / steamPerUnit. A rebuild at a standstill is 37 s at three cars (was 43), 208 s at twenty
  // (was 243). Since note 969 the blower at a stand (standingDraft) makes those 8 s and 10 s.
  "steamPerUnit": 17.55,
  "auxiliaryDrain": 0.0585,
  "heatingPerCar": 0.078975,
  "fullThrottleDemand": 0.585,

  // Holding the vent valve open dumps pressure at this rate. T97: it's how the crew slows the train (steam sets the
  // speed). T109 playtest: in the cab, on its left side, so one player can drive it all from the footplate.
  "ventRate": 12,
  // T109: a rupture (spec B.6, GDD §23) seizes the cylinders: the train sheds speed at ruptureDecel (m/s²) down to
  // ruptureCoastBelow (m/s), then coasts, to a stop unless braked. The repair kit (GDD §12; train.json kit, it rides in
  // car 1) held at the firebox for repairSeconds makes the boiler good again, cold and empty: fire it back up from nothing.
  "ruptureDecel": 1.5,
  "ruptureCoastBelow": 4,
  "repairSeconds": 25,
  // T97 playtest: no regulator. Pressure sets the speed the engine can make: none at powerFloor, the line's top speed at
  // safetyValveLift. Coal for more speed, the vent (or the brake) for less. The engine pulls at full effort until it's
  // within driveSpeedBand m/s of that speed. Its cylinders draw with the effort of pulling away or with speed, whichever
  // is more, drawing fullThrottleDemand from fullDemandAt of top speed: running flat out is the spec's "full throttle".
  "steamDrive": true,
  "driveSpeedBand": 3,
  "fullDemandAt": 0.85,
  // The director's test build (7 Oct 2026): "both heat and pressure are going down ... and I'm maintaining top speed". The
  // engine stopped pulling as the pressure fell, but nothing held the train back: on the flat it shed 0.01 m/s² (train.json
  // resistance), and with the fire out it was still at 17 m/s four minutes on. Pressure sets the speed both ways now: going
  // faster than its steam can make, the engine holds the train back at up to starvedDecel m/s², none from workingBandMin
  // up, all of it at powerFloor (ARCHITECTURE §8 note 319). Not in the spec; DESIGN-TODO: first pass. 0: it coasts as it did.
  "starvedDecel": 0.12,
  // Firebox below this fraction of capacity counts as a low fire (Hollow's trigger, GDD App. A.5).
  "lowFireFraction": 0.2,
  "startPressure": 75,
  "startFirebox": 4
}
